.TH GGE\-TWEEN 7 "generalGameEngine" "Engine Manual" "Engine Manual"
.SH NAME
gge\-tween \- timers, easing curves, float tweens, and screen shake
.SH DESCRIPTION
tween.h holds the small time\-based helpers every game needs: a \fBTimer\fR that counts down, a \fBTween\fR that animates one float along an easing curve, the \fBease_*\fR curves themselves, and a \fBShake\fR that produces a decaying random offset. All are advanced from \fBupdate\fR with dt, so they run at the fixed tick rate and are unaffected by frame rate.
.PP
.SH TIMERS
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
Timer spawn;                          /* pointer\-free: fine inside GameState */
timer_start(&g\->spawn, 2.0f);         /* start or restart */
\&
/* every tick */
if (timer_tick(&g\->spawn, dt)) {      /* true exactly once, on the tick it expires */
  spawn_enemy(g);
  timer_start(&g\->spawn, rng_between(&g\->rng, 1.5f, 3.0f));
}
if (g\->invuln.running) ;              /* still active? */
.EE
.RE
.PP
A Timer is \fB{ float left; bool running; }\fR. For many small cooldowns a bare float in the entity (\fBp\->cooldown \-= dt; if (p\->cooldown <= 0) ...\fR) is just as good; Timer makes the one\-shot expiry explicit. Timers in GameState survive saves; timers in scene statics (a title\-screen blink) do not need to.
.PP
.SH EASING
An \fBEaseFn\fR maps progress 0..1 to eased 0..1:
.TP
\fBease_linear\fR
constant speed; progress bars, fades.
.TP
\fBease_in_quad\fR
starts slow, ends fast; things leaving.
.TP
\fBease_out_quad\fR
starts fast, settles; things arriving (the default choice).
.TP
\fBease_in_out_quad\fR
slow, fast, slow; deliberate moves, camera pans.
.TP
\fBease_out_cubic\fR
like out_quad with a harder stop.
.TP
\fBease_out_back\fR
overshoots then settles; pop\-ups, punchy UI.
.TP
\fBease_out_bounce\fR
bounces to rest; dropped objects, playful titles.
They are plain functions; use them without a Tween too: \fBx = lerpf(a, b, ease_out_quad(t))\fR with \fBt = clampf(elapsed / duration, 0, 1)\fR.
.PP
.SH TWEENS
A Tween drives one float through a pointer:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
static float panel_x;                 /* presentation state */
static Tween panel_tw;
\&
/* on_enter: start off screen and slide in */
panel_x = \-200;
tween_start(&panel_tw, &panel_x, 16, 0.4f, ease_out_back);
\&
/* update */
tween_update(&panel_tw, dt);
\&
/* render */
draw_rect(rect(panel_x, 40, 180, 100), rgba(0, 0, 0, 200));
\&
/* later: slide out, then pop the scene when done */
if (closing && tween_done(&panel_tw)) engine_pop(e);
.EE
.RE
.PP
tween_start(3) records the current value as \fBfrom\fR, so retargeting a running tween is smooth. The target must outlive the tween: a scene static or a GameState float, never a local. One Tween per float: a Vec2 needs two, a color four. A tween that has finished (or was never started) reports tween_done(3); an inactive tween costs nothing to update.
.PP
.SS Chaining and sequencing
Start the next tween when the previous reports done, from update. For longer sequences, an index into a small table of (target, to, duration, ease) advanced on done is a tiny timeline. Fades between scenes are a full\-screen rectangle whose alpha is a tweened float (gge\-drawing(7)); a whole cutscene of fades, waits and moves is gge\-cutscene(7).
.PP
.SS Tweens on GameState
Tweening a gameplay value (health bar catching up to real health, a slow\-motion time scale) is fine, but the Tween struct itself holds a pointer and belongs in a scene static; after a save is loaded the tween is simply gone and the value stays where it was. For values the simulation depends on, move them explicitly with \fBlerpf\fR and \fBsmooth_t\fR each tick instead; that is stateless and saves cleanly.
.PP
.SH SMOOTH FOLLOWING WITHOUT A TWEEN
\fBvalue = lerpf(value, target, smooth_t(0.2f, dt))\fR approaches a moving target with a time constant of 0.2 s, at any tick rate, with no start or end bookkeeping. Health bars, cameras (camera_follow does this), a cursor that glides to the selected item, a speedometer needle. See smooth_t(3).
.PP
.SH SCREEN SHAKE
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
static Shake shake;
shake_start(&shake, 6, 20);           /* 6 px, losing 20 px/s \-> about 0.3 s */
/* every tick */
shake_update(&shake, dt);
cam.offset = v2(shake.ox, shake.oy);  /* or add to draw positions when there is no camera */
.EE
.RE
.PP
A bigger \fBamount\fR for bigger hits; a larger \fBdecay\fR for a shorter shake. Calling shake_start during a shake sets the new amount, so stacking hits just restart it larger. For a "rumble" that lasts, call shake_start each tick with a small amount and a huge decay. Shake through the camera offset so following and bounds are untouched (gge\-camera(7)).
.PP
.SH HIT\-STOP AND SLOW MOTION
Not a helper, but two lines: keep \fBg\->time_scale\fR (1 normally) and a \fBhitstop\fR timer; on a big hit set \fBtime_scale = 0\fR and start the timer for 0.05 s; each tick, \fBfloat sdt = dt * g\->time_scale;\fR feeds the entity ticks while UI and the timer use the real dt; when the timer expires, \fBtime_scale = 1\fR. Slow motion is \fBtime_scale = 0.3f\fR tweened back to 1.
.PP
.SH COMMON PATTERNS
.TP
\fBBlink\fR
\fBif ((int)(t * 4) % 2) draw\fR with \fBt += dt\fR.
.TP
\fBPulse\fR
\fBscale = 1 + 0.1f * sinf(t * 6)\fR.
.TP
\fBScore roll\-up\fR
tween a float \fBshown_score\fR to the real score with ease_out_quad; draw \fB(int)shown_score\fR.
.TP
\fBFade in on scene enter\fR
\fBfade = 1; tween_start(&fade_tw, &fade, 0, 0.5f, ease_out_quad);\fR and draw the black rect with \fBfade * 255\fR alpha last.
.TP
\fBRespawn countdown\fR
a Timer started at death; on expiry reset the player.
.TP
\fBCooldown bar\fR
draw \fBcooldown.left / cooldown_total\fR as a width.
.SH PITFALLS
.IP \(bu 2
Updating tweens or timers from render: they run at display rate, and twice per frame on 120 Hz screens relative to what you intended.
.IP \(bu 2
Pointing a Tween at a local variable.
.IP \(bu 2
Reading \fBtimer_tick\fR from two places: the true is delivered once.
.IP \(bu 2
Forgetting that a Tween's \fBfrom\fR is captured at start: starting one before the value is initialised tweens from garbage.
